I think dr. King's legacy is one of  leadership one of vision one of setting  a standard for how we understand who our  fellow citizens are and who we are as  Americans he spoke to all of those  issues very eloquently when I met dr.  King I was involved in a summer  mentoring program and I was in a  journalism workshop and at that time dr.  King had been named the heir previous he  was named Man of the Year by Time  magazine so everywhere he went to press  followed him so he addressed the people  who were taking part in the program I  was involved in the the mentoring  program he addressed all of the  participants it was about two or three  hundred kids in Harlem and he told them  that their what they were trying to do  to make Harlem a better place by finding  out what was wrong and trying to work on  those issues that was going to be  something that bore really serious fruit  so he spoke to us and then after the  event the press interviewed him and as a  member of the press I they gave me press  credentials and I covered the event like  I was with the press corps so it was you  know the very first person I interviewed  as a journalist was dr. King Wow you  know somebody like that touches your  life in that way it has a profound  effect and it enabled me to get more  behind what he was doing and how he was  doing it because you know there was a  debate going on as to you know was his  approach better or were other more  militant approaches better and really  the answer was that all people who were  concerned about the issues they need to  come together and deal with the issues  and work out all the other stuff later  we found that out the hard way but you  know dr. Caine was a leader in all that  so I think what he means to America is  very profound because we're still  trying to get to the point where we have  achieved all the things that he  envisioned when he said we shall  overcome my most poignant memory of dr.  King would be as he made some of the  points of some of his public speeches  this speech that he gave at the march on  Washington in 1963 is as part of  American history that's part of that is  just as important and just as profound  as the Gettysburg Address  or maybe President Roosevelt addressing  the nation after the attack on Pearl  Harbor I mean those words are etched in  our collective memory in history and our  part of what America is the things that  I learned from dr. King have to do with  perseverance and getting to the point  where you learn how to communicate with  your fellow human beings it's probably  one of the most difficult lessons that  we as humans have tried to absorb and we  keep thinking we have it figured out and  we don't we keep placing ourselves in a  position where we have to work on it and  it requires constant vigilance we get to  the point where all of a sudden we don't  like the people around us and they are  our fellow human beings and in America  they are our fellow citizens how do we  get along it should be a lot easier than  it has proven to be but hopefully we're  making progress  I think dr. King's effect on me  personally was I my ability to identify  goals and understand that it takes a  while to get to the point where you're  satisfied with what you wanted to  achieve with your goals  it's a it takes a while and watching  what he went to in his life and it ended  so tragically but he could see that  maybe America was going to get there  eventually but he didn't live to see it  so there's a lot of disappointment  involved but we can't give up on our  vision and we can't stop working for all  the things that we want to achieve dr.  King changes the trajectory of my life  by giving me a different idea about what  a life well-lived could be again he  achieved so much but the goals that he  identified we didn't get there but just  in pointing those out and helping  America identify the best way to make  our republic grow and become stronger  and one nation I think he gave us some  tools to to get there dr. King was able  to galvanize America because America saw  eventually that what he wanted was just  that black Americans did deserve  first-class citizenship it's it should  not be a difficult thing for people to  get that but it took all of his hard  work and sacrifice including dying the  way he did for America to get the  message dr. King's message has resonated  for so long it is because as Americans  it's something that we all aspire to  it's very noble it's what every country  should aspire to but as Americans we've  gotten the closest to that ideal the  whole idea of every individual being  worthy and every individual having the  right to a vote and to have his or her  opinion recorded and that justifying  their citizenship that that should be  the common experience for all people in  all nations but I think in America we've  come closest to really making it a  reality there for personal  it actually happens here Martin Luther  King jr. day sometimes allows us to pat  ourselves on the back for what has been  achieved and it allows us to ignore the  work that we have to do to get to the  real goals of first-class citizenship  for all Americans we haven't gotten  there yet  there are obvious indications that we  haven't so you know that's why I'm  critical of Martin Luther King jr. Day  because the fact that we have a holiday  does not mean that we have achieved all  of the necessary objectives of dr.  King's life work dr. King's objectives  for where America should go he was  talking about economic equality  something that we need to really be  careful about because the wealth  disparity between those who have and  those who do not have us becoming more  dramatic as time goes on us that's not a  good thing we should protect and allow  our middle class to grow that has been  the best path for our country since its  inception and I think we're getting away  from that so you know I think the  ongoing emphasis on diversity and  equality is a worthy thing for our  country and I hope we stick to it  complacency um uh you know within the  people the community that admires dr.  King they think that everything's swell  everything is not swell but that kind of  complacency is dangerous you know it's  something that allows us to let bad  things continue to fester and that's not  a good thing as far as where we are in  the art of justice because at different  perspectives people can see different  things so people who have done well and  have benefited from modern society I  would say that they see it in a lot more  positive light than people who are  struggling you know I think we still  have work to do with regard to you know  equality and equal opportunity for every  American the whole disparity in  educational opportunities and successes  you know really affects that my dream  for the next 50 years is that Americans  continue to educate the youngest among  us in more effective and you know  intuitive ways you know the whole idea  of you know how to educate young people  really is what gives us the next  generation of knowledge and innovation  and hopefully progress our educational  system really needs to keep pace with  the what's possible in terms of you know  doing a better job of educating the  youngest of others young people really  need to understand the context that dr.  King had to deal with just what America  was about as the second world war ended  and black Americans had this new  consciousness about combating racism  after seeing what happened in the effort  to eliminate fascism you know world war  two and we had the same problems here in  America you know a segment of our  population was being discriminated  against in the same way that Jews and  other minorities were discriminated  against in Nazi Germany and black  Americans saw the correlation of  everything and decided that they needed  to risk risk at all but  we needed to move forward and achieve  our own liberation and I think dr. King  was the ideal person to to lead that  movement and you know the people that  inspired him  Vernon Johns who was dr. King's mentor  those people really are still in the  shadows but they were also visionaries  and help dr. King form his vision and I  think that you know as we go forward we  should learn a little bit about that  whole context you know the people who  helped get dr. King's movement up and  running Americans can honor dr. King's  legacy by just having an honest look in  the mirror as to what we can all do to  [Music]  not give racism and bigotry the  opportunity to get a foothold on on our  public psyche on our institutions and  stuff we really need to eliminate that  it's it's a poison and it can ruin our  society some people think that just  because the poorest among us are the  ones who are the ones that suffer and  you know and that's okay it's not okay  and the ills that they have to deal with  will end up being a problem for all of  all Americans and you know in every  segment of society so I think you know  our own efforts that self-preservation  should also be part of our concern and  we need to survive as the righteous  place that our founding fathers  envisioned I had heard a lot about the  monument to dr. King in Washington DC  but you know it's kind of hard to  imagine it even from the photographs  that you might see of it beforehand but  it's magnificent you know because it  shows them just to me he is a colossus  you know in terms of what he stood for  and the moral  certainty of his vision at all it's all  there you know and just very very well  stated I think it's a it's a beautiful  Monument and people should appreciate it
 

